erdict:** I have obtained a complete solution.  \n  The only possible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\text{ or }k=1 } .\n\\]\n\n* **Method Sketch:**  \n  1.  Show that the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\) cover all required points, giving a configuration with \\(k=0\\).  \n  2.  Replace the highest horizontal line \\(y=n\\) by any non‑sunny‑parallel line; the remaining \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines together with this single sunny line still cover all points, giving a configuration with \\(k=1\\).  \n  3.  Prove that \\(k\\ge 2\\) is impossible.  \n      *   Any line that is not sunny is a **row** (\\(y=c\\)), a **column** (\\(x=c\\)) or a **diagonal** (\\(x+y=c\\)).  \n      *   Every point \\((a,b)\\) of the set  \n          \\[\n          S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n          \\]\n          lies on exactly one row, one column and one diagonal.  \n      *   A covering of \\(S_n\\) by rows, columns and diagonals needs at least \\(n\\) such lines; indeed the \\(n\\) rows already form a covering, and any covering must contain at least one line from each of the \\(n\\) rows (otherwise the points of a missing row could not all be hit, because any other line meets that row in at most one of its points).  \n      *   A sunny line can intersect each row, each column and each diagonal in **at most one** point, so it can replace at most one row, at most one column and at most one diagonal.  \n      *   If \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines are used, at most \\(n-k\\le n-2\\) non‑sunny lines remain. To cover all points we would have to omit at least two rows (or two columns or two diagonals), but each sunny line can replace at most one of them, leaving at least one point uncovered – a contradiction.  \n\nHence only \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nFor a line we use the following terminology  \n\n* **row** – a line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, i.e. \\(y=c\\);  \n* **column** – a line parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, i.e. \\(x=c\\);  \n* **diagonal** – a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), i.e. \\(x+y=c\\);  \n* **sunny** – a line that is none of the three types above.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Configurations with \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  The \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n  \\[\n  y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n\n  \\]\n\n  are distinct and none of them is sunny.  \n  For any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(1\\le b\\le n\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(y=b\\).  \n  Thus the \\(n\\) lines cover all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  The row \\(y=n\\) contains only the point \\((1,n)\\).  \n  Replace this row by any line that is not a row, a column or a diagonal (for instance the line through \\((1,n)\\) with slope \\(2\\)).  \n  The remaining \\(n-1\\) rows \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\) together with the new line still cover every point of \\(S_n\\): points with \\(b\\le n-1\\) are on the row \\(y=b\\); the point \\((1,n)\\) is on the new line.  \n  Hence a configuration with exactly one sunny line exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 2\\)\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(S_n\\) and that exactly \\(k\\ge 2\\) of them are sunny.  \nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal R,\\;\\mathcal C,\\;\\mathcal D\n\\]\n\nbe the subsets of \\(\\mathcal L\\) consisting respectively of rows, columns and diagonals; thus  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal R|+|\\mathcal C|+|\\mathcal D| = n-k\\le n-2 . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  A lower bound for the number of non‑sunny lines\n\nFor each \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the set  \n\n\\[\nR_b=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\}\n\\]\n\nis the set of points of \\(S_n\\) that lie on the row \\(y=b\\).  \nIf a row \\(y=b\\) is **absent** from \\(\\mathcal R\\), then every point of \\(R_b\\) must be covered by a column or a diagonal (or a sunny line).  \nA column (or a diagonal) meets the row \\(y=b\\) in at most one point, because it is not parallel to the row.  \nConsequently, if the row \\(y=b\\) is missing, we need at least \\(|R_b|=n+1-b\\) distinct non‑sunny lines to cover the points of \\(R_b\\).\n\nSince \\(|R_b|\\ge 2\\) for every \\(b\\le n-1\\), the only row that could possibly be omitted is the top row \\(y=n\\), whose set \\(R_n\\) consists of the single point \\((1,n)\\).  \nTherefore **every row \\(y=b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n-1\\) must belong to \\(\\mathcal R\\).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal R|\\ge n-1 . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  What a sunny line can replace\n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line.  \nBecause \\(L\\) is not parallel to any row, column or diagonal, it meets each row in at most one point, each column in at most one point and each diagonal in at most one point.  \nThus a single sunny line can replace **at most one** row, **at most one** column and **at most one** diagonal.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Deriving a contradiction\n\nFrom (2) we have \\(|\\mathcal R|\\ge n-1\\).  \nIf \\(k\\ge 2\\) then \\(n-k\\le n-2\\), so at most \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines are available.  \nBecause \\(|\\mathcal R|\\ge n-1\\), at least one of the rows \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\) is **not** present in \\(\\mathcal R\\).  \nLet \\(y=b\\) be such a missing row. All points of that row must be covered by columns, diagonals or sunny lines.  \nA column (or a diagonal) can meet the row \\(y=b\\) in at most one point, and a sunny line can also meet it in at most one point.  \nSince the row \\(y=b\\) contains \\(n+1-b\\ge 2\\) points (because \\(b\\le n-