 a missing row we need at least \\(n+1-b\\) distinct lines among the non‑sunny lines and the sunny lines.  \n   *Since there are only \\(n\\) lines in total, the only rows that can possibly be omitted are those whose length does not exceed the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines; i.e. rows with \\(b\\ge n+1-k\\).  \n   *Consequently at most the top \\(k\\) rows may be absent. The remaining \\(n-k\\) rows must be present, so at least \\(n-k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are non‑sunny.  \n   *Thus the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(k\\); i.e. any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n\\).  \n   *Because we have exhibited a construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), these are exactly the possible values.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** when it is **not** parallel to any of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\text{horizontal }(y=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{vertical }(x=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{diagonal }(x+y=\\text{const}).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Constructions\n\nWe show that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Case \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n .\n\\]\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(1\\le b\\le n\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(y=b\\).  \nAll \\(n\\) lines are non‑sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n*Case \\(k\\ge 1\\).*  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-k\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of the lowest \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines (all non‑sunny).  \nThe rows that are not in \\(R\\) are  \n\n\\[\ny=n-k+1,\\;y=n-k+2,\\dots ,y=n .\n\\]\n\nEach of these rows contains respectively \\(k,\\,k-1,\\dots ,1\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nDefine the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;y=x+(n-k) \\qquad\\text{(slope }1\\text{, hence not parallel to any forbidden direction)} .\n\\]\n\nFor \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\) the point  \n\n\\[\nP_j=(j,\\;n-k+j)\n\\]\n\nbelongs to the row \\(y=n-k+j\\) and satisfies \\(y=x+(n-k)\\); therefore all the points\n\\(P_1,\\dots ,P_k\\) lie on \\(L\\).  Consequently \\(L\\) covers **one** point from each of the\n\\(k\\) omitted rows.\n\nThe remaining points of those rows are precisely the points \\((a,b)\\) with\n\\(b\\in\\{n-k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and \\(a\\neq b-(n-k)\\).  \nThere are  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}-k=\\frac{k(k-1)}{2}\n\\]\n\nsuch points.  They can be covered by the \\(k-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=k-1 .\n\\]\n\nIndeed, a vertical line \\(x=c\\) meets each of the omitted rows in at most one point,\nand the \\(k-1\\) different values of \\(c\\) give enough distinct intersections to\ncover all \\(\\frac{k(k-1)}{2}\\) points (because \\((k-1)k\\ge \\frac{k(k-1)}{2}\\) for every\n\\(k\\ge1\\)).  \n\nThus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L = R\\;\\cup\\;\\{L\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=k-1\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers every point of \\(S_n\\), and has\nexactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the line \\(L\\) and the \\(k-1\\) vertical lines).  \nHence a configuration exists for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Necessity of \\(k\\le n\\)\n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(S_n\\) and contains\nexactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Let  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal R,\\;\\mathcal C,\\;\\mathcal D\n\\]\n\nbe the subsets of \\(\\mathcal L\\) consisting respectively of rows (\\(y=\\text{const}\\)),\ncolumns (\\(x=\\text{const}\\)) and diagonals (\\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)).  \nThus  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal R|+|\\mathcal C|+|\\mathcal D| = n-k . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed row \\(y=b\\) denote  \n\n\\[\nR_b=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\},\n\\qquad |R_b| = n+1-b .\n\\]\n\nIf the row \\(y=b\\) is **absent** from \\(\\mathcal R\\), every point of \\(R_b\\) must be\ncovered by a line from \\(\\mathcal C\\cup\\mathcal D\\cup\\) (the set of sunny lines).\nA column, a diagonal or a sunny line is not parallel to the row, therefore it can\nmeet the row in **at most one** point.  Consequently, covering all points of\n\\(R_b\\) requires at least \\(|R_b| = n+1-b\\) distinct lines among\n\\(\\mathcal C\\cup\\mathcal D\\) together with the \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nHence a row can be omitted only when its length does not exceed the number \\(k\\) of\nsunny lines; i.e. we must have  \n\n\\[\nn+1-b\\le k\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad b\\ge n+1-k .\n\\]\n\nTherefore at most the top \\(k\\) rows (those with indices \\(n-k+1,\\dots ,n\\))\nmay be missing from \\(\\mathcal R\\).  All the remaining \\(n-k\\) rows must be present,\nso  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal R|\\ge n-k . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal C|+|\\mathcal D| = (n-k)-|\\mathcal R|\\le (n-k)-(n-k)=0 ,\n\\]\n\nwhich forces \\(|\\mathcal C|=|\\mathcal D|=0\\).  Thus the only non‑sunny lines are the\n\\(n-k\\) rows, and the remaining \\(k\\) lines are sunny.  In particular we must have\n\\(k\\le n\\); no configuration with more than \\(n\\) sunny lines can exist because we\nare limited to exactly \\(n\\) lines.\n\nCombined with the constructions of §1, this shows that the admissible values of\n\\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion ###\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of possible numbers of sunny lines in a\nfamily of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with\n\\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
