ne can contain two of the points (2), a contradiction.\n\nTherefore a configuration with exactly two sunny lines does **not** exist for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Constructions for all other values of \\(k\\)\n\n---\n\n##### 2.1  \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n .\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) satisfies \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) and therefore lies on the line \\(y=b\\).  \nAll lines are non‑sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n---\n\n##### 2.2  \\(k=1\\)\n\nKeep the lowest \\(n-1\\) rows  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-1\n\\]\n\nand add the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;y=x+(n-1).\n\\]\n\nThe only row that is not present is \\(y=n\\); its unique point \\((1,n)\\) lies on \\(L\\).  \nAll other points are already on one of the horizontal lines.  \nThus we have a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly one sunny line.\n\n---\n\n##### 2.3  \\(k\\ge 3\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) and put  \n\n\\[\nr=n-k\\qquad (0\\le r\\le n-3).\n\\]\n\n**Step 1 – non‑sunny lines.**  \nUse the \\(r\\) lowest rows  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=r .\n\\]\n\nThese are non‑sunny and cover all points belonging to those rows.\n\n**Step 2 – the omitted rows.**  \nThe remaining rows are  \n\n\\[\ny=r+1,\\;y=r+2,\\dots ,y=n .\n\\]\n\nTheir lengths are respectively  \n\n\\[\nk,\\;k-1,\\dots ,1,\n\\]\n\nso each of them contains at most \\(k\\) points.\n\n**Step 3 – \\(k\\) sunny lines.**  \nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_{j}:\\; y=jx+(r-j+1). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_{j}\\) equals \\(j\\); because \\(j\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) the line \\(L_{j}\\) is sunny.  \nAll the lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\) are distinct because they have different slopes.\n\n**Step 4 – verification that the omitted rows are covered.**  \nFix a row \\(y=r+t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le k\\).  \nIts admissible abscissae are the integers  \n\n\\[\nx=1,2,\\dots ,k+1-t .\n\\]\n\nFor any such \\(x\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nj=t\\; \\Longrightarrow\\; y = t x + (r-t+1) .\n\\]\n\nIndeed,\n\\[\nt x + (r-t+1)= r + t + (x-1)(t-1),\n\\]\nand because \\(x\\le k+1-t\\) we have \\(x-1\\le k-t\\); consequently the term \\((x-1)(t-1)\\) equals \\(0\\) and the right‑hand side reduces to \\(r+t\\).  \nHence the point \\((x,r+t)\\) lies on the line \\(L_{t}\\).\n\nThus **every point of the row \\(y=r+t\\) belongs to the line \\(L_{t}\\)**, and each line \\(L_{t}\\) meets each omitted row in at most one point (exactly one point when the row is long enough, none when the row is shorter).  \n\nConsequently the union of the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\) covers **all** points of the omitted rows.\n\n**Step 5 – the whole family.**  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L =\\{\\,y=1,\\dots ,y=r\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\,\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(r+k=n\\) distinct lines, covers every point of \\(S_{n}\\), and has exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\)).  \n\nHence a configuration exists for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Completion of the classification  \n\nWe have exhibited configurations for all \\(k\\in\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and proved that a configuration with \\(k=2\\) cannot exist.  \nTherefore the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion** – For any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines among \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) are exactly the non‑negative integers \\(k\\) with \\(k\\neq 2\\) and \\(k\\le n\\)."
