tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Each line has slope \\(i\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore all \\(k\\) lines are sunny.\n   For a point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) set  \n\n   \\[\n   i = b-a+1\\;(1\\le i\\le k).\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting this value of \\(i\\) into (1) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   b = (b-a+1)a + (k+1-(b-a+1))\n      = a b - a^{2}+a + k+1-b+a-1\n      = b,\n   \\]\n\n   so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_{\\,b-a+1}\\).  Hence every required point belongs to exactly one of the lines \\(L_i\\).  Thus for \\(n=k\\) we have a configuration with \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Induction step.*  \n   Suppose a configuration \\(\\mathcal C_n\\) for some \\(n\\ge k\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covers all points with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  Add the new horizontal line \\(y=n+1\\) (a non‑sunny line).  The enlarged family \\(\\mathcal C_{n+1}\\) now has \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, still exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, and it covers all points with \\(a+b\\le n+2\\).  Repeating this step yields a configuration for every \\(n\\ge k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   Consequently, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable family of \\(n\\) lines exists.\n\nCombining the three parts we obtain the complete description of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of points that have to be covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The case \\(k=2\\) is impossible\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(S_n\\) and that exactly two of the lines are sunny.  \nDenote the two sunny lines by \\(U_1,U_2\\) and the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines by \\(\\mathcal N\\).\n\n*Claim 1.*  A sunny line meets each horizontal row \\(y=r\\) in **at most one** point.  \nIndeed, if a sunny line contained two points \\((a,r)\\) and \\((a',r)\\) of the same row, its slope would be \\(0\\), contradicting the definition of sunny.\n\nConsequently each row \\(y=r\\) contains at most two points of \\(U_1\\cup U_2\\).\n\n*Claim 2.*  Every non‑sunny line meets a given row in **at most one** point.  \nA non‑sunny line is either horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\).  \nA horizontal line meets a row in all its points, but then the whole row is covered by a single non‑sunny line.  \nA vertical line and a line of slope \\(-1\\) intersect a given row in exactly one point.\n\nHence, apart from the rows that are themselves horizontal members of \\(\\mathcal N\\), the points of any other row must be supplied by the two sunny lines and by at most \\(|\\mathcal N|\\) vertical/diagonal lines, i.e. by at most \\(n-2\\) distinct points.\n\nThere are \\(n\\) rows altogether.  Since \\(|\\mathcal N|=n-2\\), at most \\(n-2\\) rows can be horizontal members of \\(\\mathcal N\\).  Therefore **at least two rows are not horizontal non‑sunny lines**.  \n\nLet those rows be \\(y=r_1\\) and \\(y=r_2\\) with \\(r_1<r_2\\).  \nTheir lengths are  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\{(a,r_i)\\in S_n\\}\\,| = n+1-r_i\\ge 2 .\n\\]\n\nThus each of the two rows contains at least two points that are not covered by a horizontal non‑sunny line.  \nAll points of a row that are not covered by a horizontal line must be supplied by the two sunny lines (at most two points per row by Claim 1) and possibly by vertical/diagonal lines.  Since we have already used all \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines as horizontal lines, **no vertical or diagonal non‑sunny line remains**.  Consequently each of the two rows can receive at most two points from the two sunny lines, i.e. at most four points in total.\n\nBut the two rows together contain at least  \n\n\\[\n( n+1-r_1 ) + ( n+1-r_2 ) \\ge 3+2 =5\n\\]\n\npoints (the smallest possible values are \\(r_1=n-1,\\;r_2=n\\), giving rows of lengths \\(2\\) and \\(1\\); together with the point \\((1,n+1)\\) that belongs to the next row, we obtain at least five points).  Hence the five points cannot be covered by the two sunny lines, a contradiction.\n\nTherefore a configuration with exactly two sunny lines does **not** exist for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Constructions for the remaining values of \\(k\\)\n\n#### 2.1  \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n .\n\\]\n\nAll are non‑sunny and together they contain every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  \\(k=1\\)\n\nUse the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\) and the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\; y = x + (n-1) .\n\\]\n\nThe horizontal lines cover all points except \\((1,n)\\); the line \\(L\\) passes through \\((1,n)\\) and is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor the line \\(x+y=0\\).  Exactly one line is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  \\(k\\ge 3\\)\n\nWe first treat the case \\(n=k\\).\n\n**Lemma 3.**  For every integer \\(k\\ge3\\) the \\(k\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = i\\,x + (k+1-i)\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,k)\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nare sunny and together cover all points of \\(S_k\\).\n\n*Proof.*  The slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(i\\); since \\(i\\ge1\\) we have \\(i\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), so each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_k\\).  Because \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n1\\le b-a+1\\le k .\n\\]\n\nSet \\(i:=b-a+1\\).  Substituting this value of \\(i\\) into (2) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= i\\,x + (k+1-i)\\\\\n  &= (b-a+1)a + (k+1-(b-a+1))\\\\\n  &= ba - a^{2}+a + k+1-b+a-1\\\\\n  &= b .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_i\\); consequently every point of \\(S_k\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(L_i\\).  ∎\n\nHence for \\(n=k\\) we have a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n**Inductive step.**  \nAssume a configuration \\(\\mathcal C_n\\) for some \\(n\\ge k\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covers \\(S_n\\).  Add the horizontal line \\(y=n+1\\) (which is non‑sunny).  The enlarged family \\(\\mathcal C_{n+1}\\) now consists of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, still exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, and it covers all points of \\(S_{n+1}\\) because the new horizontal line supplies precisely the points with \\(b=n+1\\).  Repeating this step yields a configuration for every \\(n\\ge k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nConsequently, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Final classification\n\nFrom Sections 1 and 2 we obtain:\n\n* No configuration exists with exactly two sunny lines.\n* Configurations exist for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\).\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) a configuration exists.\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. all non‑negative integers not exceeding \\(n\\) except \\(k=2\\). ∎\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
