"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to obtain a complete classification of the admissible values of\u202f\\(k\\).  I can prove that  \n\n* \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are possible for every \\(n\\ge 3\\);  \n* \\(k=n\\) is possible when \\(n\\) is odd.  \n\nThe existence (or impossibility) of configurations with any other number of sunny lines remains open.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   *\\(S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\\)* is the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).\n\n2. **\\(k=0\\).**  \n   The \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) are all non\u2011sunny and each point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).\n\n3. **\\(k=1\\).**  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with the sunny line \\(y=x-(n-1)\\).  \n   All points with abscissa \\(\\le n-1\\) are covered by a vertical line; the unique remaining point \\((n,1)\\) lies on the sunny line.\n\n4. **\\(k=n\\) for odd \\(n\\).**  \n   Write \\(n=2m+1\\).  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y = i + (x-1)\\,d_i ,\\qquad \n   d_i=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   m+1-i &\\text{if }1\\le i\\le m+1,\\\\[2pt]\n   m+2-i &\\text{if }m+2\\le i\\le n .\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n   * The slope \\(d_i\\) is an integer and never equals \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\); hence every \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \n   * The line \\(L_i\\) passes through the point \\((1,i)\\) (so the \\(n\\) points of the first column lie on distinct lines).  \n   * For any column \\(x=j\\;(1\\le j\\le n)\\) the set \\(\\{\\,i+(j-1)d_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\,\\}\\) equals \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n+1-j\\}\\).  Consequently every lattice point \\((j,b)\\) with \\(b\\le n+1-j\\) belongs to exactly one of the lines \\(L_i\\).\n\n   Hence the family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines and covers all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n5. **Open part.**  \n   The arguments above give explicit configurations for \\(k=0,1\\) (all \\(n\\)) and for \\(k=n\\) when \\(n\\) is odd.  No construction (or impossibility proof) is known for the intermediate values of \\(k\\); the original problem therefore remains unsolved."
